{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 16, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 14, "high": 16, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": 0, "high": 1, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 1, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 1,
      "why": "Full pipelining gives 1 result/cycle, so Fmax >= 32 MHz is the only way to meet throughput; the 32-burst in 0.44 us needs ~32 cycles, so N+2 <= 32 (N <= 30) is fine. Accuracy <= 2^-12 needs N ~ 14-16 with W ~ 16-20; round is the only way to hit the tight error bound. This is the area-cheapest family that can meet the throughput constraint."
    },
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 16, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 14, "high": 16, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": 0, "high": 1, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 1, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["round"]},
        {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 4, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 1,
      "why": "Registering every m stages trades FFs for a slightly higher Fmax (shorter combinational path per stage) and lower latency (ceil(N/m)+2). With N=14-16 and m=2-4, latency stays well under 32 cycles, so the 0.44 us batch bound is met; this can beat pipelined on luts_plus_ffs if the Fmax gain is real. Same accuracy box as pipelined since accuracy is family-independent."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "No facts yet, so I seed the two families I own. The spec's throughput >= 32 MS/s forces 1 result/cycle, which rules out iterative/unrolled_k and leaves only pipelined and pipelined_m. The 0.44 us batch bound for 32 results means the CORDIC must finish a burst in <= 32 cycles, so latency <= 32 is the binding system constraint; both families satisfy this for N <= 30. Accuracy <= 2^-12 with round rounding is achievable at N ~ 14-16 and W ~ 16-20, so I focus the search there to keep area low while leaving headroom. I split the budget evenly between the two families: pipelined is the area floor, pipelined_m explores the FF/Fmax trade-off that may push the front further. angle_guard and frac_guard are kept small (0-1) since the error budget is tight and extra guard bits mostly cost area without helping the 2^-12 bound; rounding is fixed to round because truncation typically doubles the error and would risk the constraint."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "The Pareto front is well-established and feasible. The minimum area design (pipelined_m, 1072 LUTs+FFs) meets all constraints: throughput (97.8 >= 32), accuracy (12.3 bits > 12), and system latency (0.169 us <= 0.44 us). The front covers the trade-off space effectively from 1072 to 2266 LUTs+FFs and 12.3 to 14.6 accuracy bits. Since this is the final round, we stop and select the design with minimum luts_plus_ffs (1072) as per the selection rule."
,
  "next_plan": null
}
